    "media:imagetext": "(Family Letter No. 6)\nEn route Singapore-Batavia\nDear Family,\nWe are flying along the coast of Sumatra\nwith Batavia,\nor I should say Jakarta, two hours away. The people of the\nUnited States of Indonesia in their swing away from the Dutch\ntoward extreme nationalism have changed the name of world-known\nBatavia to Jakarta and the U.S.I. Post Office has announced\nthat after April Ist all mail addressed to \"Batavia\" will be sent\nto the dead letter office! In like manner, and about as sensible,\nthe Philippines are talking about the abolition of English and\nSpanish in favor of Togolug - the most used of some eighty Phil-\nippine dialects.\nIt is as though our forebearers had abolished\nEnglish ar er the Revolution and adopted Choctaw or some other\nIndian language. Ridiculous, of course, but it shows just how\nfar these people will go in their almost fanatical netionalism.\nSingapore, in the extreme south of the Malay Peninsula\nis only sixteen miles north of the equator.\nIt is on an island\nbought by the British from the Sultan of Johore. Here the\nvessels of the seven seas must pass through the narrow Straits\nof Malacca, under the guns of the British who take a modest toll\nfrom each\nand every passerby.\nDuring the war the British\nMalaya to first resist Japanese invasion and then occupation.\nGuns and ammunition were smuggled in all along the extensive\ncoastline, and many promises were made by the military com-\nmanders few of which were kept by the political bigwigs after\nthe war was won.\nAs a result there grew up in Malaya the\n\"Peoples Anti-British Army\" which is\na motley collection of\npatriots, communists and bandits which has terrorized the entire\npeninsula and paralyzed business especially the large rubber\nplantations.\nApparently the leaders are determined to make life\nso tough for their white masters that none will wish to stay.\nNothing short of a major military operation can succeed against\nthem.\nThat would be costiy both in money and lives, but the\nBritish have no choice but to clean up the entire peninsula or\nget out, and I can't understand why the Labor Government con-\ntinues to temporize and take less than the obviously required\nmeasures. One thing sure, if \"Winnie\" gets back in control he\nwill soon put an end to the open season on Englishmen.\nHe will\ngo part way back to the good old buccaneering days of Victoria,\nthe East India Company and Raffles of Singapore.\nThis mornings Singapore paper has a story of bandits\nwho stormed a rubber plantation, killed the manager, burned\ndown all the buildings, ten trucks and seven jeeps.\nwe saw a\ntrain pull into Singapore with two flat cars\neach equipped with\nan armored pillbox in which British soldiers man machine guns.\nHowever, the bandits derail the trains, tear up the tracks and\nto drive very far beyond the island and most all of the plan-",
